# Patch numbers are permanent IDs: this file alone sets the order, and merged
# patches keep the number of the one they absorbed the others into. A patch's
# name starts with the part of Samba it changes; the headings say why.
#
# Whole source files that do not exist upstream live in overlay/, mirroring the
# Samba tree, and are copied in before the first patch (patch_copy_overlay).
# Patches change upstream files only; never add or edit an overlay file here.
#
# The patch files are generated: edit them through build/samba4x_replay.py
# (init, amend, export, verify), never by hand.
#
# Sections, in order:
#   1. Fixes to stock Samba
#   2. Upstream proposals
#   3. Build and toolchain
#   4. NetBSD compat
#   5. NetBSD bugs
#   6. macOS workarounds
#   7. Apple firmware coexistence
#   8. Diagnostics

# 1. Fixes to stock Samba.
# Each applies to pristine Samba, builds on Linux without the overlay, needs
# no later patch, and fixes a bug that a stock build with default options can
# reach on a platform Samba supports.
#
# open_file_ntcreate() found no file under the name, but a directory appeared
# before the open: the O_CREAT|O_EXCL open in fd_open_atomic() failed with
# EEXIST and its retry without O_CREAT opened the directory for reading, which
# open(2) allows. smbd then held a file handle on a directory (is_directory
# false, so a later QUERY_DIRECTORY failed). Return
# NT_STATUS_FILE_IS_A_DIRECTORY instead, so the caller opens it as a directory,
# as for one found at lookup time; the descriptor stays as a path reference for
# open_directory() to reopen, as mkdir_internal() leaves its new directory. A
# file replaced by a directory is already refused by the dev/ino check before
# it.
0063-smbd-open-file-created-directory.patch|open a name that became a directory as a directory
# smbd_calculate_maximum_allowed_access_fsp() returned full access for root
# (a share with "force user = root", as on the appliance) before taking write
# access away for a read-only share or a read-only file, so opening a
# read-only file with MAXIMUM_ALLOWED asked for write access and the open
# refused it with ACCESS_DENIED (smbtorture
# smb2.maximum_allowed.read_only_file). Root now skips only the ACL check.
0066-smbd-maximum-access-root-read-only.patch|maximum access for root still honours read-only shares and files
# fruit_pwrite_meta() copies a short AfpInfo write (17-59 bytes at offset 0)
# into a 60-byte stack buffer and stores all 60 bytes, so the stream kept
# uninitialized stack bytes after the caller's data. Zero the buffer first.
0067-fruit-pwrite-meta-zero-fill.patch|zero-fill short AfpInfo writes
# xattr_tdb_setattr() returns without freeing its stackframe when it cannot
# lock the row; the caller's frame then frees it out of order ("Freed frame
# ..., expected ..." at level 0, a panic in DEVELOPER builds). It and
# xattr_tdb_removeattr() also return -1 without setting errno when the row
# cannot be parsed (EINVAL) or stored (the store's status as an errno).
0068-xattr-tdb-setattr-lock-failure-frame.patch|free the stackframe and set errno on every xattr_tdb write failure
# streams_xattr_unlinkat() returned the positive error code from
# streams_xattr_get_name(), returned 0 when it could not open the base file,
# and reported success when removing the stream's xattr failed for any reason
# other than ENOATTR. Return -1 with errno set in every failure.
0069-streams-xattr-unlinkat-errors.patch|report every failed stream unlink
# nt_time_to_unix_timespec_raw() cast the seconds to time_t unchecked. With a
# 32-bit time_t (the NetBSD 4 lanes) a time after 2038-01-19 03:14:07 wrapped
# before nt_time_to_full_timespec() compared it with TIME_T_MAX: a client
# setting 2040-02-05 got 1903-12-30 stored, and 2106-02-07 got 1969-12-31.
# Saturate at time_t's range, so the TIME_T_MAX/TIME_T_MIN clamps apply.
0071-util-time-32bit-time-t-saturate.patch|saturate NTTIME conversions at a 32-bit time_t
# Since 4.23 file_find_subpath(), which decides a directory rename without
# "strict rename", skips a POSIX open below the directory only when the
# directory's own handle is POSIX too. vfs_fruit marks a Mac's opens POSIX in
# openat, but a Mac opens the directory it renames with read attributes,
# delete and synchronize access only, which open_directory() serves without
# reopening it, so that handle keeps the lookup's non-POSIX flag. Every rename
# of a folder holding a Mac's open files failed with ACCESS_DENIED, Time
# Machine's rename of the sparsebundle it has just attached included (upstream
# bug 15926, which its fix left broken from 4.23 on). Skip POSIX opens below
# whatever the directory handle is, as 4.22 did and "strict rename" does;
# other opens below still refuse the rename, as on Windows. The same check
# decides SMB1 and trans2 renames and, with strict rename off, whether a
# directory with only hidden entries left can be deleted; 4.22 skipped POSIX
# opens there too. The opens below keep their old names, which a durable
# disconnect writes into the cookie its reconnect looks the file up by, and
# which delete on close unlinks: after the parent's share mode lock is released,
# rename this smbd's opens below with the bookkeeping of a file rename, comparing
# whole UTF-8 parent names, and broadcast the rename (MSG_SMB_FILE_RENAME with
# old_base_name) so other smbds rename theirs, from their share root. The rename
# is done on disk by then, so a failure there only logs.
0076-smbd-dir-rename-posix-opens-below.patch|a directory rename skips POSIX opens below it
# delay_for_oplock_fn() waits for a lease break already under way before it
# asks whether the open holding the lease is stale. An open that conflicted
# with a live durable open sends the break and defers; the holder's smbd marks
# the lease breaking, finds its client gone and disconnects the durable open,
# which wakes the deferred open. The retry no longer conflicts, so nothing is
# left to break, but the lease is still breaking: it defers again and waits
# out the 70-second timeout, though the stale entry was dropped in the same
# traverse and no one could acknowledge the break (smbtorture
# smb2.durable-open.open2-lease, when the open arrives before the old smbd has
# seen its client leave). Ask whether the entry is stale before that wait, as
# the code after it does. Disconnected durable entries are unaffected: their pid
# sorts them first, where the lease and write-lease checks above already ask
# whether they are stale, as stock does.
0078-smbd-open-stale-breaking-lease.patch|no wait on a breaking lease whose open is stale
# A file with no xattr_tdb row has an empty attribute set: xattr_tdb_getattr()
# reports ENOATTR for it, not EINVAL, to every caller of the library. Stock
# vfs_xattr_tdb's fgetxattr already maps its errors to ENOATTR; EINVAL escapes
# through getxattrat (async dosmode) and to the library's other callers, such
# as the metadata migrator. Since 0038, HFS shares do not use this library.
0018-xattr-tdb-missing-row-empty.patch|xattr_tdb missing rows behave like empty xattrs
# macOS/iOS Photos writes many assetsd metadata streams. Once xattr_tdb's
# stream-name list exceeds Samba's initial 1024-byte listxattr buffer, return
# -1 with ERANGE, the retry signal listxattr(2) gives, instead of the length it
# needs, which callers take for bytes written into the too-small buffer. A probe
# with size 0 still gets the length.
0027-xattr-tdb-listxattr-erange.patch|xattr_tdb retries oversized xattr name lists
# With xattr_tdb:ignore_user_xattr, user xattr reads pass to the next module
# before xattr_tdb has built its local smb_fname copy, so upstream passes NULL.
# Pass the caller's name instead.
0047-xattr-tdb-getxattrat-bypass-name.patch|pass the caller's name when xattr_tdb bypasses a user xattr read
# macOS Time Machine sends FSCTL_SRV_REQUEST_RESUME_KEY inside a compounded
# request while probing backup-disk capabilities. The default VFS finishes that
# read at once but delivers its callback from an immediate event, so fruit's
# offload_read wrapper and smb2_ioctl_network_fs each keep their parent request
# in progress, and the SMB2 compound guard rejects it with
# NT_STATUS_INTERNAL_ERROR. Run the callback right away when the subrequest has
# already finished, there and in vfs_time_audit's wrapper.
0028-ioctl-resume-key-sync-completion.patch|propagate synchronous resume-key IOCTL completion
# aio_child_cleanup() retires a helper idle for 30 to 60 seconds through
# aio_child_destructor(), which makes it exit by writing one byte with no
# descriptor. The helper's read_fd() then declares a descriptor array of
# length 0, which C does not allow for a variable-length array (UBSan reports
# it in the helper). Size the array MAX(num_fds, 1).
0033-aio-fork-empty-control-message.patch|avoid zero-length arrays during aio_fork shutdown
# A helper's failed pread returns -1, which the parent casts to an oversized
# read and turns into EIO; return the helper's errno instead.
0052-aio-fork-read-errno.patch|keep the helper's errno for a failed read
# A shutdown close from a dropped connection or a bare SMB2 LOGOFF runs from
# whatever working directory the last request left, which is "/" after any tree
# disconnect (close_cnum) or another share's root; a tree disconnect itself
# changes into its share first. Delete on close then finds no parent directory
# and leaves the file (upstream's wide-links check stops it resolving anywhere
# else), and 0029's by-name stat refuses the durable cookie. Change into the
# file's own share root first. A disk 0041 finds revoked stays safe even when
# Apple remounted it at the same path: both steps fstat the old descriptor
# before any lookup by name, and that fails on a revoked vnode.
0062-smbd-shutdown-close-share-root.patch|shutdown closes resolve names from the file's share root
# The parent waits for a new scavenger's hello with no time limit, and serves
# nothing meanwhile (no new connections, no reaping): a child that never says
# hello stops the parent for good. Wait at most 30 seconds
# (SCAVENGER_HELLO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS), and kill a child whose hello times out or
# cannot be read. Only the expiry that started it is lost, and later ones start
# another scavenger.
0075-smbd-scavenger-hello-timeout.patch|bound the parent's wait for a new scavenger's hello
# streams_xattr's extent code takes ENODATA to mean that an optional extent is
# missing, but VFS xattr calls report ENOATTR. Linux builds never see the
# difference, since lib/replace/system/filesys.h defines ENOATTR as ENODATA
# there. On BSD and macOS, which define ENODATA separately, a missing extent
# fails a stream read, the cleanup after a shrinking write and a stream delete
# after the primary xattr is gone. Check for ENOATTR.
0035-streams-xattr-enoattr.patch|missing stream extents report ENOATTR
# smbd_smb2_advance_incoming() reads a frame whose NBT type is not 0x00 in full
# into a buffer allocated on the request, ignores it, and reuses the request
# for the next read. The request is freed only after the next SMB2 request
# completes, so every ignored frame's buffer stays allocated until then. Free
# each one when its frame is ignored.
0079-smbd-ignored-frame-free-buffer.patch|free each ignored frame's buffer
# brl_reconnect_disconnected() frees br_lck on each of its four failure
# returns, but its caller, share_mode_do_locked_brl_fn(), owns br_lck: after a
# failed reconnect it reads br_lck->modified from the freed memory, then frees
# br_lck again, and talloc aborts with "Bad talloc magic value". Leave br_lck
# to its owner.
0080-locking-reconnect-br-lck-double-free.patch|leave br_lck to its owner after a failed reconnect
# share_mode_forall_entries() drops stale share entries: disconnected opens and
# opens of a dead smbd that another open finds, and the opens the scavenger
# expires. Each keeps its lease in leases.tdb for good. On the appliance the
# lock directory is a small RAM disk, and a full one makes client smbds fail
# opens and abort. Collect the dropped entries' leases and, after the store,
# remove each one no remaining entry holds, as a close does in
# remove_share_oplock(). share_mode_cleanup_disconnected() removes an expired
# open's lease itself; drop that, or it would find the lease already gone and,
# for a durable open, warn and report failure.
0081-locking-stale-entry-lease.patch|a share entry dropped as stale takes its lease with it
# A durable reconnect that fails after smb2srv_open_recreate() stores its open
# back with disconnect time 0 and no expiry, and keeps the lease and the
# byte-range locks it has taken over, so nothing expires or releases them.
# Store the open disconnected with its own disconnect time and schedule the
# scavenger, as close_durable() does; give up the locks it took over
# (brl_drop_reconnected()) and remove its lease unless another open holds it.
# The scavenger finds the share mode entry by the record's file id, which stock
# leaves zero for a file the open created: fsp_bind_smb() stores the record
# before the file has an id. smbd_smb2_create_after_exec() stores the file's id
# with the rest of a durable open's record (cleanup_ph() and net serverid read
# it too).
0082-durable-failed-reconnect-cleanup.patch|a failed durable reconnect stores its open disconnected and gives back its locks and lease
# Only an entry's own smbd removes its open records, so an smbd that crashed or
# was killed leaves them until smbd restarts, and a reconnect refuses such a
# record. After an unclean exit cleanupd asks the scavenger (an empty
# MSG_SMB_SCAVENGER) to walk the open records and release those of exited
# smbds; brl_cleanup_disconnected() and
# cleanup_disconnected_share_mode_entry_fn() accept an owner that no longer
# exists. Clean exits, ordinary reconnects included, trigger no walk. An open
# whose record has no file id (a non-durable open of a file it created, 0082)
# loses only its record; its share mode entry goes when another open finds it
# stale. scavenger_cleanup() logs NT_STATUS_CANNOT_DELETE (an open still in
# use, such as one reconnected before its timer fired) at debug level, where
# stock logs a warning, and the scavenger ignores a message of the wrong size.
0077-smbd-release-orphaned-durable-opens.patch|release durable opens left by dead smbds

# 2. Upstream proposals.
# Changes upstream would have to agree to; they need no appliance patch.
#
# Time Capsule lock databases live on a tiny RAM disk. Add an smb.conf
# max-dead setting for every local dbwrap TDB (dbwrap_tdb_max_dead:*). Our 0
# sends volatile lock-database deletes straight to the freelist instead of
# retaining thousands of per-hash-chain dead records after Time Machine closes
# files; persistent TDBs already use 0, so only a positive value changes them.
0017-dbwrap-tdb-max-dead.patch|configurable dbwrap TDB max-dead records
# Native HFS xattrs hold at most 3,802 bytes, so smb.conf sets 35 xattrs per
# stream for Windows alternate data streams of up to about 130 KiB. Upstream
# accepts at most 16.
0048-streams-xattr-max-64-per-stream.patch|allow up to 64 xattrs per stream
# Refactor only: pread, pwrite and fsync have three copies of the same
# send/done/recv code; use one path that takes the command (and one recv for
# pread and pwrite).
0053-aio-fork-single-request-path.patch|one request path for pread, pwrite and fsync
# aio_fork:max_children bounds each tree connection's helpers (0 keeps
# upstream's no limit). Requests wait in a short FIFO for a free helper and are
# dispatched oldest first; past the FIFO bound, or when fork() fails, a read or
# write runs in the client's own smbd, which slows that client instead of
# failing its I/O. An fsync, which can take long, queues past the bound. The
# first request past it logs the saturation at level 0, once until the FIFO
# drains. Each helper's shared buffer is 8 MiB, Samba's default SMB2 read and
# write size: stock aio_fork refuses anything over 128 KiB with EINVAL. Stock's
# name for WRITE_CMD in debug output, "PRITE", becomes "WRITE".
#
# Only aio_fork_send (for a request queued alone) and the event loop (a tevent
# immediate, as tevent_queue does) run the FIFO: freeing, completing or failing
# a request never dispatches or completes another. A request freed while its
# helper works (smbd's teardown via assert_no_pending_aio, or a caller freeing
# it) leaves the helper busy until it replies; the reply is dropped and the
# helper reused (retired only if that read fails), as pthreadpool_tevent
# orphans a job, but by handing the helper the pending read instead of refusing
# the free. So a teardown forks, runs and completes nothing, and frees each
# helper once with its tree connection; stock aio_fork panics (SMB_ASSERT) when
# a busy helper is freed. A helper stuck in a hung read keeps its slot
# until the read returns, as a pool thread would.
0031-aio-fork-max-children.patch|bounded aio_fork helpers with FIFO backpressure

# 3. Build and toolchain.
# Samba marks some generated tools as hostcc but still lets waf inherit
# target compiler/linker settings. Keep those host tools on the VM compiler,
# with the host's flags and none of the target's link markers.
0001-waf-hostcc-target-flags.patch|waf hostcc isolation from target flags
# Apply static link flags only to the static binaries we build: the deployed
# smbd (with its link map), the other shipped binary, the metadata migrator,
# by name, and the opt-in tc_*_test regression drivers, which never ship.
0049-waf-static-appliance-binaries.patch|static link flags for smbd, the migrator and regression drivers
# Upstream repeats several typedefs across headers (SMB_STRUCT_STAT and
# files_struct, Heimdal's heim_pcontext and krb5int32). C11 and GCC 4.6 and
# later accept a repeated typedef; the NetBSD 4 SDK's GCC 4.1.2 rejects it, so
# guard each one.
0014-includes-typedef-guards.patch|duplicate typedef guards
# NetBSD 7's <glob.h> (the NetBSD 6 lane's SDK) declares glob_pattern_p(),
# which conflicts with bundled popt's static fallback of the same name ("static
# follows non-static"); rename popt's copy. The NetBSD 4 headers lack the
# declaration.
0016-popt-glob-symbol-rename.patch|bundled popt glob symbol rename
# Samba uses C11 generic selection for const-preserving charset helpers. The
# existing NetBSD SDK GCC predates that syntax; retain the types with GNU
# builtins.
0036-charset-legacy-gcc-generic.patch|const-preserving charset helpers on legacy GCC
# waf writes each configure test program exactly as the check gives it, and
# some end in indentation instead of a newline. GCC 4.1 reports "no newline at
# end of file", an error under -Werror, so on the NetBSD 4 SDK such a check
# failed whatever it tested. The one that mattered adds the stack protector
# and _FORTIFY_SOURCE: NetBSD 4 binaries were built without both. End every
# test program with a newline.
0073-waf-config-test-final-newline.patch|configure test programs end with a newline

# 4. NetBSD compat.
# What Samba needs to build and run on Apple's NetBSD 4 and NetBSD 6 kernels,
# their HFS disks and the device's small memory. None of these works around a
# NetBSD bug; section 5 has those.
#
# 4a. Kernel, libc and SDK gaps.
# Neither Apple kernel has the *at system calls: NetBSD 4 and NetBSD 6 both
# return ENOSYS for openat, fstatat, mkdirat, unlinkat, readlinkat, renameat,
# linkat, symlinkat, mknodat and utimensat, although the NetBSD 7 SDK declares
# them. libreplace emulates them for the appliance (overlay
# lib/replace/tc_at_emulation.c): each call fchdir()s to the directory
# descriptor, makes the path-based call and changes back, which needs a process
# without threads. A rename or link between two directories runs in one of them
# and names the other relative to it ("../" up to their common ancestor, then
# down), so it works past PATH_MAX (1,024 bytes) as long as getcwd() can name
# both (4,095 bytes). futimens is emulated with futimes on both: NetBSD 4 has
# none, and NetBSD 6's ignores the call when atime is UTIME_OMIT and sets the
# mtime to -1 when mtime is. NetBSD 4 also lacks fdopendir (emulated there by
# opening "." from inside the directory and reading the whole directory up
# front, because NetBSD 4's HFS loses a listing's place whenever a descriptor on
# the directory is closed; rewinddir reads it again). On NetBSD 6, whose HFS
# keeps a listing's place but repeats its last entries when read again after the
# end, readdir keeps reporting the end until rewinddir. NetBSD 4 also lacks
# arc4random_buf (for Heimdal's libtommath) and getline (overlay
# lib/replace/tc_netbsd4_compat.c), and the O_DIRECTORY and AT_* constants.
# system/filesys.h maps each name onto the emulation, and build/_samba4x.sh
# refuses a binary that still links libc's *at stubs.
0002-replace-appliance-at-emulation.patch|*at emulation for the appliance kernels and NetBSD 4 libc gaps
# NetBSD 4 has no posix_spawn (no spawn.h). Samba's configure never defines
# HAVE_POSIX_SPAWN, so gate on the lane (TC_SAMBA4X_NETBSD4_COMPAT), not on
# that; Apple's NetBSD 6.0 kernel has posix_spawn(2), and the NetBSD 6 lane
# uses it. Both callers only start helpers the appliance does not ship, so on
# NetBSD 4 a stub fails without forking. local_np.c is live: every pipe but
# srvsvc (served inside smbd, 0005) tries to start samba-dcerpcd, and the stub
# fails with ENOENT, as posix_spawn does on NetBSD 6. queue_process.c only has
# to compile, as printing is disabled; its stub returns -1, the caller's check.
0015-spawn-netbsd4-missing-helper.patch|no helper start on NetBSD 4, which has no posix_spawn
# NetBSD 4 lacks O_CLOEXEC, which tdb_wrap passes to tdb_open; define it as 0
# in tdb_wrap.c only (tdb_open_ex() sets FD_CLOEXEC itself). A global define
# would make other code skip its fcntl(FD_CLOEXEC) fallback.
0007-tdb-wrap-o-cloexec.patch|O_CLOEXEC for tdb_wrap on NetBSD 4
# On every NetBSD lane, read the interfaces from sysctl(NET_RT_IFLIST), decoded
# by each message's RTM_VERSION, as the native service does (overlay
# lib/socket/tc_netbsd_iflist.c). libc getifaddrs() misparses Apple's NetBSD 4
# if_msghdr (152 bytes against the SDK's 144), and Samba's SIOCGIFCONF
# replacement assumes fixed-size ifreq rows, which that kernel does not return.
# A netmask is read as its address's family: the kernel trims its trailing zero
# bytes and can leave its own family unset. Only Heimdal's krb5 address lookup
# still calls getifaddrs(), for Kerberos client logins a standalone smbd never
# makes, so _samba4x.sh leaves configure's interface probe results as detected.
0043-interfaces-netbsd-route-iflist.patch|parse Time Capsule interfaces from routing messages
# 4b. No-pthread build.
# No Time Capsule kernel has pthreads. 0051 lets tevent and pthreadpool_tevent
# build without them, 0013 adds --disable-pthread, and 0022 drops
# talloc_stack's exit check in such a build.
#
# Without HAVE_PTHREAD, pthreadpool_tevent still uses pthread mutexes for its
# per-event-context glue, and tevent_threads.c's context destructor, which only
# a pthread build installs, locks a mutex. Guard those on HAVE_PTHREAD. The
# poll_mt backend needs no pthread and stays registered; nothing here selects
# it. Pthread builds are unchanged.
0051-tevent-no-pthread-build.patch|build tevent and pthreadpool_tevent without pthread
# Disable pthread before libreplace constructs the dependency graph and define
# __thread away. --disable-pthread alone already selects the synchronous
# pthreadpool backend; _samba4x.sh also passes --disable-pthreadpool so
# source3/wscript does not add vfs_aio_pthread. Without threads,
# thread-local storage is process storage, and in the static appliance binary
# any real __thread access from Samba's -fPIC objects calls static libc's
# __tls_get_addr, which only aborts. tevent_req_create (tevent's call-depth
# state) and the winbind SID lookup (libwbclient's client name) both reach
# such a variable, so without this smbd aborts at startup. The NetBSD 4
# compilers do not enable __thread; only the NetBSD 6 lane's compiler does.
0013-configure-disable-pthread.patch|configure --disable-pthread; __thread defined away
# Without pthread, talloc_stack registers an atexit handler that reports every
# stackframe still open at exit() at level 0 (and panics in DEVELOPER builds):
# about three lines for each exiting smbd connection child, plus smbd -V and
# -b. Those are the frames of the code that called exit(), not leaks; builds
# with pthread never check them, since key destructors do not run at exit().
# The handler also reads a NULL tracker when talloc_stackframe_exists()
# registered it before any frame existed. Register no handler.
0022-talloc-stack-no-exit-handler.patch|no stackframe check at exit without pthread
# 4c. HFS file growth.
# HFS has no sparse files, so growing a file allocates every block up to its
# new end. smbtorture's smb2.rw.invalid writes one byte just below MAXFILESIZE
# (16 TiB); on both devices that write never finished, and it hung or rebooted
# them. A write, SET_INFO end-of-file or server-side copy that would grow a
# file on HFS by more than the volume's available space now fails with
# DISK_FULL, as on Windows, before anything is allocated. Growth of up to 64
# MiB past the size smbd last saw needs no extra system call.
0065-smbd-hfs-growth-disk-full.patch|refuse HFS file growth beyond the available space with DISK_FULL
# 4d. smbd without helper processes.
# Samba runs on the device as the smbd binary alone, about 10 MB of a 15 MB
# RAM disk, on a device with little memory. Stock smbd forks resident helpers
# (notifyd, cleanupd, the durable-handle scavenger) and a datagram-socket sweep
# every 15 minutes, and serves RPC pipes from the separate samba-dcerpcd and
# rpcd_* binaries. Here smbd is the parent, one child per client connection
# and, when VFS_AIO_FORK_ENABLED is on, aio_fork's helpers.
#
# Finder and smbclient -L list shares through IPC$ \PIPE\srvsvc; serve that
# one pipe inside smbd (overlay tc_embedded_srvsvc.c). Other pipes (lsarpc,
# samr, wkssvc, winreg) fall through to local_np_connect, which fails to start
# the absent samba-dcerpcd.
0005-rpc-embedded-srvsvc.patch|embedded srvsvc named pipe support
# On appliance builds (TC_SAMBA4X_APPLIANCE), run notifyd and cleanupd in the
# parent, as interactive smbd does. Skip the datagram-socket sweep, which forks
# each time it runs; cleanupd still removes each exited child's socket. This
# saves two resident smbds (about 2.5 MB RSS each, some of it shared with the
# parent). The scavenger is still forked, on the first disconnected durable
# handle, as upstream does: expiring a handle waits for that file's share-mode,
# byte-range and open-record locks, which a client smbd stuck in the kernel
# can hold indefinitely, and in the parent that wait would stop it accepting
# connections. Forked helpers work without pthread.
0008-smbd-helpers-in-parent.patch|host smbd helper services in the parent event loop

# 5. NetBSD bugs.
# Workarounds for bugs in Apple's NetBSD 4 and NetBSD 6 kernels and their HFS.
# Both releases are long out of support, so none of these is for upstream.
#
# Apple's NetBSD 4 and NetBSD 6 kernels lose writes to a static binary's
# initialized data: handling the first write to a .data page, UVM fault-ahead
# maps the neighbouring pages from the executable again, dropping this process's
# (or its parent's) changes to them. Globals silently return to their initial
# values; talloc_magic does, so talloc aborts with "Bad talloc magic value" in
# forked children and elsewhere, depending only on code layout. A first-priority
# constructor in talloc, linked into every Samba binary, turns fault-ahead off
# for the writable segment with MADV_RANDOM, and talloc_lib_init repeats it
# where NetBSD 4's GCC has no constructor priorities.
0046-talloc-data-faultahead.patch|keep static-data writes: no UVM fault-ahead on the writable segment
# Apple's NetBSD 6 kernel shows a child the writes its parent makes after
# fork() to pages whose reference bit was cleared before it: its ARM
# pmap_protect() skips the invalid PTEs that reference emulation leaves, so
# their PVF_WRITE survives and the parent's next write makes the shared page
# writable without a copy-on-write fault (a connection child can spin forever
# on a half-updated copy of the parent's context). NetBSD 4's pmap is not
# affected. On the NetBSD 6 lanes (TC_FORK_REPAIR), libreplace includes overlay
# lib/replace/tc_fork_repair.c: linker wrappers keep a registry of private
# mappings, and right before each fork() every private mapping, the stack too,
# is protected PROT_NONE and back (dropping the stale page state). Touching a
# page is not enough: one left writable that way stays shared at every later
# fork(). The registry records read-only and PROT_NONE private ranges too, so
# that one mprotect() later makes writable is repaired from then on. The native
# service and rsync link the same file.
0070-replace-fork-repair.patch|fork() repair for Apple's NetBSD 6 kernel
# HFS on the device can report file and directory owner/group as 4294967295.
# Normalize only that sentinel in init_stat_ex_from_stat(), so ACL, stream and
# security descriptor paths see the forced root/wheel appliance identity. The
# core-file directory setup calls lstat() directly, bypassing that
# normalization, so on appliance builds it uses the non-strict create, which
# skips both the owner and the 0700 checks; panics then produce disk-backed
# cores under logs/cores/smbd.
0023-stat-hfs-unknown-owner.patch|HFS unknown owner in corepath setup and stat metadata
# Ignore st_blocks drift on every durable reconnect: Time Machine's sparsebundle
# bands on HFS change their allocation-block count while the handle is
# disconnected. The other identity checks stay strict, and the mismatch is
# logged at level 1, tagged TIMECAPSULE_DURABLE_ST_BLOCKS_MISMATCH.
0019-durable-allow-block-count-drift.patch|allow durable reconnect across HFS allocation-block drift
# HFS on the device can update ctime when the last writable fd closes. Record
# durable cookies after that close, so the reconnect check keeps comparing
# ctime strictly, against the metadata after the close rather than a stale
# snapshot. The post-close stat is by name, so it relies on 0062's share-root
# working directory.
0029-durable-post-close-cookie-stat.patch|record durable cookie stat after fd_close

# 6. macOS workarounds.
# Workarounds for how macOS clients, Time Machine above all, reconnect and
# send requests.
#
# macOS reconnects SMB2 durable opens before the old smbd has finished tearing
# down the lost connection and marked them disconnected; on these devices that
# teardown can take over a minute on a slow disk. Check that live record like a
# disconnected one and, for the same durable open only, defer the create and
# retry every 150 ms until 90 seconds after it arrived (upstream's poll-open
# deferral, made callable from smb2_create.c), instead of reporting the handle
# as missing. Deferring rather than sleeping keeps this smbd reading its
# socket: a sleeping smbd lets its TCP window close, and macOS answers that
# with short WRITEs (0072). FILE_NOT_AVAILABLE is only the internal "still
# busy" signal: once the 90 seconds run out the client gets
# OBJECT_NAME_NOT_FOUND, as upstream and MS-SMB2 3.3.5.9.12 give for an open
# still attached to a session (macOS revokes the file on any reconnect error).
# A reconnect of an open this same smbd holds gets that answer at once, as
# stock gives it: only this smbd's own LOGOFF could release that open during
# the wait, and macOS reconnects on a new connection, which another smbd serves.
# Keep durable-v2 handles 300 seconds so early releases survive while later
# opens finish closing, but grant the timeout the client asked for, as upstream
# does (at most 300 seconds, 60 for none): macOS fails Time Machine's reconnect
# timeout setting unless the grant matches its request, and never uses the
# grant to decide a reconnect.
0024-durable-open-live-retry.patch|defer live durable-open reconnects for up to 90 seconds
# Workaround for a macOS bug: macOS sends each request through a kernel call
# with a 5 s send timeout that reports success when the timeout cuts the send
# short, so after the server's TCP window has stayed shut a WRITE can arrive
# with its data short and the client's next requests inside the data; smbd
# would write them into the file and answer success. Refuse such a WRITE before
# dispatch, without writing or answering it: the client resends it after a
# reconnect, an error reply to a WRITE fails Time Machine, and not reading
# makes the client's next send time out. Parse on from where the next request
# starts, answer everything sent after the WRITE, and once nothing else is
# outstanding and nothing has been read or written for 500 ms (so a request
# sent in answer to the last reply is read, not cut off by the FIN) end the
# connection with a FIN, at most 60 s after the catch: macOS fails the WRITE
# 120 s after it began sending it, which was before the stall that cut it. A
# request the client sends unprompted in the last moment before the FIN can
# still cross it unanswered.
# On a Time Machine mount macOS refuses to reconnect while a CREATE, CLOSE,
# LOCK, or a delete, rename or security SET_INFO waits for its reply, so those
# must not be discarded. A WRITE counts as short only when its data holds a
# request header with the WRITE's session ID and a message ID inside the credit
# window that is not the WRITE's own, followed only by such requests to the end
# of the PDU; a correct client never sends that, so its WRITEs are written as
# sent. The client can send fewer bytes after the cut than the WRITE is
# missing and then wait for replies, so a WRITE is checked as it arrives, on
# each read, acting once a request after the cut has wholly arrived. Where the
# next request starts cannot be found, or after 8 matching headers that no
# requests follow (only a hostile client sends those), discard everything and
# end the connection the same way. Only sends over macOS's 2 KiB send
# low-water mark can be cut. Not checked: encrypted sessions (a cut encrypted
# WRITE fails decryption and smbd drops the connection), WRITEs with a request
# chained after them (the chained request would pass for a cut; macOS cuts
# only WRITEs sent alone), compounds such as the CREATE/WRITE/CLOSE macOS uses
# for extended attributes, WRITEs read by receivefile (logged at startup if
# min receivefile size is set), and every WRITE when multichannel is on
# (0072's state is one per smbd; logged at startup).
0072-smbd-short-write-desync.patch|refuse a short macOS WRITE, answer what follows, then end the connection
# A Mac reconnecting names its lost session in the new session setup and
# gives that request 60 seconds. Upstream answers only once the old session's
# smbd has closed it, with no time limit, so an smbd that cannot run (a long
# HFS close, a wait for a lock) kept the Mac from connecting at all. Wait at
# most 50 seconds, then answer; 0024 then defers the durable reconnects that
# follow until the old smbd releases the opens.
0074-smbd-session-close-previous-timeout.patch|answer session setup after 50 seconds while the previous session still closes

# 7. Apple firmware coexistence.
# Samba shares the disks with Apple's own servers and follows the firmware's
# handling of them.
#
# 7a. Native Mac metadata.
# Apple firmware stores AFP FinderInfo and ordinary Mac xattrs in native HFS
# attributes that its SMB server reaches only through private xattr syscalls
# (overlay airport_native_xattr.h), and resource forks in the file's resource
# fork, reached as <file>/..namedfork/rsrc. Store them there so AFP and SMB see
# one copy: fruit owns FinderInfo and resource-fork stream semantics, xattr_tdb
# owns ordinary native attributes.
#
# On an HFS share, xattr_tdb keeps ordinary xattrs and Apple's com.apple.*
# streams in native attributes instead of its database, and leaves FinderInfo
# and resource forks to fruit. A value HFS cannot hold (over 3,802 bytes) fails
# with EINVAL, as Apple's AFP server fails it, not E2BIG, so the client gets
# NT_STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER rather than ACCESS_DENIED. A Mac's com.apple.*
# stream never gets that far: smbd max xattr size (3,802) makes streams_xattr
# refuse it first, with the same EINVAL (0054). What does reach the kernel's
# E2BIG is a Windows client's NT ACL, EA or reparse data.
0038-xattr-tdb-native-hfs.patch|native HFS attributes for xattr_tdb
# Apple's com.apple.* streams are one native attribute without the extent count
# byte that streams_xattr stores, so allow that byte and refuse a larger value
# instead of splitting it across extents: AFP must see the whole attribute. The
# refusal is EINVAL, as for 0038's. Windows streams keep their extent layout.
# streams_xattr, xattr_tdb and the migrator tell the streams apart with one
# classifier (tc_apple_stream_native_len() in airport_native_xattr.h).
0054-streams-xattr-native-apple-streams.patch|whole native attributes for Apple streams
# On an HFS share fruit ignores fruit:metadata and serves the AFP_AfpInfo
# stream from the native com.apple.FinderInfo attribute (overlay
# tc_fruit_native_finderinfo.c); an all-zero value counts as absent, as it does
# for AFP. It also skips AppleDouble conversion there: fruit never reads ._
# files on HFS. The deploy migrator converts them, but only on a volume that
# still has a legacy xattr.tdb (every TimeCapsuleSMB version that wrote ._
# files also wrote one), which saves a whole-disk walk on every deploy.
# Uninstall removes the payload with that database, so ._ files on a disk
# uninstalled and reinstalled stay unconverted and hidden. A FinderInfo the
# stream list cannot read leaves AFP_AfpInfo out of it, as stock fruit does for
# unreadable metadata, instead of failing the whole list.
0055-fruit-native-finderinfo.patch|native HFS FinderInfo in fruit
# Building on 0055's native_hfs switch, fruit serves the AFP_Resource stream
# from the file's native resource fork, opened as <file>/..namedfork/rsrc like
# AFP does (overlay tc_fruit_native_rsrc.c). On an HFS share fruit:resource is
# ignored. Deleting the stream empties the fork of a regular file only:
# truncate() would follow a symlink to its target's fork.
0056-fruit-native-resource-fork.patch|native HFS resource forks in fruit
# The static helper in overlay tc_xattr_hfs_migrate.c moves legacy
# TDB/AppleDouble metadata during deploy only, on every deploy while legacy
# databases remain; it merges several historical databases without changing
# source mtimes and keeps anything it cannot prove orphaned. Values larger than
# a native attribute stay in legacy storage as completed metadata. It sets
# aside (never deletes) databases whose remaining rows are proven orphans or
# such values, keeps those values' ._ files, and deletes only fully migrated
# databases.
0057-xattr-hfs-migrate-build.patch|build the deploy-only metadata migrator
# 7b. Apple disk rebinding.
# Apple can revoke a bumped USB disk's vnode and remount the same dkN. Record
# each tree's volume identity at connect, forward the manager's reload signal
# from the parent to every worker, and disconnect only trees whose disk binding
# was revoked, replaced or removed; other shares on the session stay connected
# (overlay source3/smbd/tc_disk_bindings.c).
0041-smbd-stale-tree-reload.patch|forward parent reloads and disconnect only revoked Apple disk bindings
# 7c. Native symlinks.
# Apple's AFP server and SSH store real POSIX symlinks on these disks, while
# macOS SMB clients create 1067-byte Minshall+French "XSym" files. Keep the
# disk native (tc:native symlinks, set by the unified service's smb.conf).
# Existing XSym files stay as they are; there is no migration.
#
# List native links to every SMB client as symlink reparse points, and treat a
# terminal link as the object for macOS (it resolves links itself and renames
# without FILE_OPEN_REPARSE_POINT). A link is opened as itself without a
# descriptor and stat'ed with lstat; its target is mapped into the client's
# namespace (an absolute target is kept verbatim); AAPL clients get the reparse
# tag, not max_access, in EaSize; and non-Mac clients see a link to a directory
# inside the share with FILE_ATTRIBUTE_DIRECTORY (the target resolved as smbd
# follows links, as stock's dangling-link check does). A link's reparse tag is
# SYMLINK without reading the link: a listing asks for it for every link.
0045-smbd-native-links-reparse-points.patch|native POSIX symlinks as reparse points
# Map catia's private-use characters in a link's own name when links are read
# and created.
0058-catia-native-link-names.patch|catia name mapping for link reads and creates
# Keep a link's streams, xattrs and times on the link itself, never its
# target: the native HFS backend reaches a link handle, which has no
# descriptor, by path, and stream deletion skips links. vfs_default's path
# fallbacks never follow a link, on every share: chown and times act on the
# link itself, while mode, flags and xattr writes fail with ENOTSUP, an xattr
# read with ENOATTR, and a listing is empty. catia maps the handle's name for
# xattr calls, as for its other fsp calls, so the backend's path is the disk's.
0059-smbd-native-links-own-metadata.patch|a link keeps its own streams, xattrs and times
# When the creating client explicitly closes the only handle to a file it
# created (a shutdown close never converts), replace an XSym file, or a
# placeholder carrying a symlink reparse payload (Windows, Linux
# symlink=native/nfs/wsl), with a native link under the share mode lock, never
# replacing another object, and carry its metadata over except Finder info; a
# file with a resource fork stays a file. The original is removed only after its
# fd closes, and each conversion ends with sync(): without it the devices' HFS
# journal can panic ("jnl: start_tr: active_tr is NULL", a kernel bug that
# then fires in whichever process ends the next transaction).
# A Mac (AAPL) reading a native link's data gets the equivalent XSym bytes,
# whoever made the link, and writes that leave them unchanged succeed, so a Mac
# still caching the XSym file it wrote keeps working. Other reparse tags (FIFOs,
# sockets, devices, junctions) are refused; mklink /D stays refused by
# upstream's regular-file check. Only non-Mac connections see
# FILE_SUPPORTS_REPARSE_POINTS, so macOS keeps creating XSym.
0060-smbd-native-links-convert-on-close.patch|new SMB links become native links when their creator closes them

# 8. Diagnostics.
# Log each step of a client's durable reconnect at level 3, prefixed
# tc_reconnect: (SMBD_DEBUG_LOGGING shows it): negotiate with its client GUID,
# the session setup and the previous session it names, why close_previous acted
# or left that session alone, the old smbd closing it, each durable open it
# disconnects, the reconnect request and its result, and why connections end.
0061-smbd-reconnect-diagnostics.patch|durable reconnect diagnostics at log level 3
# The parent logs, in one line, a child smbd that exited with an error status
# or that a signal ended, and whether it dumped core; stock logs neither. A
# child that dies leaves its opens to the scavenger's rescan (0077), and this
# line says which one died and how.
0083-smbd-child-exit-logging.patch|log a child that exited with an error status or a signal
